@flexiarg test-registry/judge-adequacy
@title The Reviewer's Work Is Adequacy, Not Execution
@keywords adequacy, reviewer-judgement, coverage-gaps, author-is-not-reviewer, R9
@audience reviewers, review-lane designers
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-16 (claude-7; from NOTE-test-registry "What the reviewer does instead of rerunning"; implementation test_registry.clj `review` requires a different :reviewer and a substantive :adequacy note)
@references [test-registry/bind-warrant-to-the-diff test-registry/spot-check-at-the-declared-rate hygiene/receipt-then-gate]
@see-also buffer-cleaner/exempt-the-in-use
@why rerunning the author's tests never supplied what a separate reviewer is for: whether these are the right tests, whether they pin the behaviour claimed, whether they would catch the likely bug. On 2026-09-16 claude-4's review of the WM-11 selection posterior found the committed score added F where the Lean subtracts it and added a sign-falsifier test — adequacy work; the rerun of the author's five tests contributed nothing to that catch.
@how after a passing record check, the reviewer records an adequacy judgement: the behaviours the change claims, which tests pin each, named coverage gaps, and any tests the reviewer adds (which are new tests and carry their own warrant). Adequacy is a recorded judgement, never inferred from test success. The reviewer is a different agent from the author (checked, not assumed). The adversarial-fixture discriminator — construct the case the check is named for and watch whether it is caught — is a recommended reviewer HABIT, ruled NOT a registry acceptance rule (Joe, 2026-09-19: "that is a habit not something I want enshrined in code... I don't want the registry to become yet another red-tape machine"). It lives as guidance in the workspace CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md; a reviewer may practice it, but a warrant is never refused solely for lacking it. Three same-day catches of controls that were green while not testing what their name says: a WM-01 margin floor of gap > 1e-297 (satisfied by any nonzero double), a float-enclosure control, and a run-validity checker whose denylist let fields hide under any unlisted key.
@violation-signature "tests pass" offered as the review; an adequacy note with no named behaviours or gaps; the author as reviewer; a coverage gap noticed and silently converted into a passing claim.
! conclusion: The reviewer records which claimed behaviours the tests pin and which they miss; that judgement, not a rerun, is the review.

  + context: The record check has established that the author's run covers the diff.

  + IF:
    The reviewer must decide whether the tested change is acceptable.

  + HOWEVER:
    Green tests are the easiest thing to report and the least informative about whether the right thing was tested.

  + THEN:
    Record adequacy explicitly, with named gaps and any added tests.

  + BECAUSE:
    Independence is preserved by moving the reviewer's effort from duplication to judgement; R9 (proposal is not witness) is strengthened, not weakened.

  + evidence: zai-7's 2026-09-14 review named three gaps (unknown-kind sorry-cells, cross-JVM determinism, empty-organs fixture) as recorded judgements; WM-11 review fix futon2 74a118c5 (sign test).
